# The width and sign of what an `@` parameter points at, checked against
# the header by the compiler itself (design.md §4.19, §1.12; CLAUDE.md
# §12; panel 103, 2026-09-03; M-robustness-guards step 3).
#
# **The hole.** `extern function getline(@line: ptr, @n: i32, stream: ptr)`
# against `size_t *n` built at exit 0 with five warnings and ran: `getline`
# wrote 8 bytes into a 4-byte slot, and `--sanitize` saw nothing (panel 096
# found it, panel 103 measured it on three clangs). The spec's own advice —
# *`u64` where it says `size_t`* — passed through a pointer is
# `uint64_t *` against `size_t *`, which on macOS is `unsigned long long *`
# against `unsigned long *`: two types, one width. So neither clang's
# warning nor the strict flag can be the check: the flag refuses the
# correct `@n: u64` on one platform and never looks at sign at all
# (`-Wpointer-sign` is a warning on every clang). The check has to be the
# compiler's, and it has to ask the header.
#
# **How the compiler learns the header's type.** C11 has no
# parameter-position `__typeof__`; a `transparent_union` redeclaration is
# free and uniform but fails with `conflicting types` the moment any
# parameter is `ptr`; DWARF is cgo's route and the CI's Linux leg has no
# `llvm-dwarfdump`. What works on clang 18, 21 and 22 alike, measured: one
# translation unit that includes the program's headers and declares
# `extern __typeof__(getline) hero_ty_getline;` per extern with a numeric
# `@`, dumped with `-ast-dump=json -ast-dump-filter=hero_ty_` — 6–8 KB, 0.02 s,
# the k-th `ParmVarDecl`'s `qualType` is the header's own text for the
# parameter (`size_t *restrict`). The JSON is not the verdict: it is only
# where the TYPE TEXT comes from. The verdict is asked of clang in a
# second, tiny unit — two `_Static_assert`s per parameter, `#line`d at the
# author's declaration, `sizeof` and the sign of `(T)-1` — so it is C11
# arithmetic on the header's own type and never a width table this
# compiler carries. A failure comes back as a `static assertion failed`
# line carrying this file's marker, and `emit/ffi_pointee.hero` turns it
# into `ffi_parameter_type` on the parameter, with a `guess` fix naming the
# header's width.
#
# **Cached like an object, and published like one** (defect 134). The dump
# and the check run once per (compiler, declarations, the words they compile
# under, what the headers resolve to and hold; `cli/compiling`, defect 168),
# under `build/pointee-<key>/`, a directory every program with the same asks
# and the same headers shares. The
# record `dump.deps.txt` names the headers the dump read and, by their
# digest, the dump's answer `dump-<digest>.json` and the verdict
# `verdict-<digest>-<unit>.txt`, and it is published last (`cli/deps`,
# `cli/publish`), so a header that changes re-asks the question and an
# unchanged one costs a read. Everything here was written in place until
# 2026-09-30: a verdict emptied by a build killed while writing it failed
# every build of every program sharing the directory after it, with
# `internal error:` and nothing after the colon.
#
# **Two things this file deliberately does not do.** It does not cast: the
# emitter passes `(void *)&x` for the argument it has checked here
# (`emit/ops.hero`, `emit/extern_probe.hero`), which is what makes a
# correct binding compile clean under `-Werror=incompatible-pointer-types`
# on every clang. And it does not fail a build the dump could not serve: a
# name the header spells as a macro cannot be `__typeof__`'d, the probe
# already refuses that name on its own line, and a check that fell over
# there would be the compiler blaming itself for the author's mistake.

use ast
use source
use check/state
use ir/containers
use emit/externs as emit_externs
use cli/toolchain
use cli/libraries
use cli/compiling
use cli/process
use cli/publish
use cli/digest
use cli/deps
use cli/header_types
use cli/pointee_ask
use cli/pointee_wants
use cli/strings
use cli/read_back
use cli/kept
use emit/ffi_lookup
use emit/ffi_mutable
use parse
use resolve
use checker
use ir/lower

## The check itself

# Ask the header, once per (compiler, declarations, words, what the headers
# resolve to), the C compiler included: the dump and the verdict are clang's
# (defect 122).
# Absent is the answer when nothing is asked, when the answer is cached
# and fresh, or when the check passed; a failure carries clang's own words,
# which `emit/ffi.hero` reads back into the author's diagnostic.
# `answered` is filled with what the header said each ask points at, so the
# caller can hand it to `emit/ffi.hero`'s `explain`: a failure on an
# incomplete pointee names the TYPE and not the parameter (defect 010), and
# only this file knows which parameter asked about which type. It is filled
# on the cached path too — from the dump the cache kept — because a cached
# failure is explained the same way a fresh one is.
function check(t: toolchain.Toolchain, p: containers.Program, c: state.Checked, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], @answered: [pointee_ask.Answered], probe: compiling.Probe) -> ()?
    wanted = pointee_wants.pointees(p, c, s, decls)

    if wanted.len() == 0
        return ok()

    # Headers that do not preprocess: the build's own compile says so on the
    # author's line, and this check has nothing to add.
    if probe.read.is_err()
        return ok()
    headers = probe.headers
    words = probe.words
    memo: {str: str} @ probe.digests
    asked: [str] @ []

    for one in wanted
        asked @ asked.push(
            one.name + ":" + one.param + ":" + one.index.to_str() + ":" + one.declared + ":" + one.file + ":" + one.line.to_str()
        )

    # **The key names every word the two units compile under, from the list
    # their lines are built from, and what the headers resolve to and hold**
    # (defect 168, `cli/compiling`). It named the package's compile words
    # (defect 161) and the compiler's flags a probe takes (defect 163) beside
    # the asks, whose declaring file is the same `<heroes library>` in every
    # program: so the library's own asks, judged beside a header whose macro
    # renames one of them, replayed that refusal onto a program in another
    # directory with no such header, measured 2026-10-02 both ways on one
    # `build/`.
    key = digest.digest(
        t.fingerprint + "|pointee|" + t.compiler_key + "|" + asked.join(",") + "|" + headers.join(",") + "|words:" + libraries.key_text(words) + "|reads:" + probe.read.must() + "|" + t.runtime_key
    )
    dir = t.build + "/pointee-" + key
    listed = dir + "/dump.deps.txt"
    known = deps.vouched(listed, @memo)

    # **The verdict is keyed by the check unit's own text, not only by what
    # was asked.** Until 2026-09-04 it was `verdict.txt` under a key of
    # (compiler fingerprint, asks, headers, search, runtime) — and the
    # fingerprint is a constant, so a compiler that learned to WRITE the
    # assertion differently (defect 010's guard) replayed a verdict the old
    # compiler had reached about the old unit, measured: the repaired
    # compiler answered exit 2 on the defect's own reproducer until
    # `build/pointee-*` was cleared. It carries the record's digest as well,
    # since the check unit compiles the same headers the dump read.
    if !known.is_err()
        kept_json = kept.read(dump_of(dir: dir, sum: known.must()))

        if !kept_json.is_err()
            types = header_types.types_of(kept_json.must(), wanted)
            sugar = header_types.sugar_of(kept_json.must(), wanted)
            answered @ header_types.answered(wanted, types: types, sugar: sugar)
            verdict = kept.read(
                verdict_path(dir: dir, sum: known.must(), check_text: header_types.check_unit(headers, wanted, types: types, sugar: sugar))
            )

            if !verdict.is_err()
                if verdict.must() == "ok\n"
                    return ok()
                return fail(code: "clang", msg: verdict.must())
    process.mkdir_all(dir)?

    # Both units are published under the digest of their own text and
    # compiled from there, so a copy another build put there is these
    # bytes; clang's outputs are this check's alone.
    dump_text = header_types.dump_unit(headers, wanted)
    dump_c = unit_path(dir: dir, stem: "dump", text: dump_text)
    publish.write_whole(path: dump_c, text: dump_text, keyed: true)?
    depfile = publish.private_name(dir + "/dump.d")
    dump_json = publish.private_name(dir + "/dump.json")
    err_path = publish.private_name(dir + "/dump-stderr.txt")
    line: [str] @ [
        "clang"
        "-fsyntax-only"
        "-Xclang"
        "-ast-dump=json"
        "-Xclang"
        "-ast-dump-filter=" + header_types.PREFIX
        "-MD"
        "-MT"
        "dep"
        "-MF"
        depfile
    ]
    line @ strings.with_all(list: line, more: words)
    line @ line.push(dump_c)
    ran = process.run(program: "clang", words: line, out: dump_json, err: err_path)

    # Read back as every word clang writes is (`cli/read_back.hero`, defect
    # 337): a dump refused whole for one byte that is not UTF-8 skipped this
    # check, which stands between a wrong `@` width and the stack. Apple clang
    # 21 writes the byte of a `#line` name as U+FFFD here, measured 2026-10-05.
    json = read_back.words(dump_json)
    publish.discard(dump_json)
    publish.discard(err_path)
    rows = deps.rows_of(depfile: depfile, skip: dir + "/", @memo)
    publish.discard(depfile)

    # The headers did not compile in isolation, or a name is a macro
    # `__typeof__` cannot take: the build's own compile reports it on the
    # author's line, and this check has nothing to add.
    if !ran.started || ran.code != 0 || json.is_err()
        return ok()

    if rows.is_err()
        return fail(code: "failed", msg: "cannot record what the pointee check read: " + failure_note(rows))
    listing = deps.pointer_text(rows.must())
    sum = listing.slice(from: 0, to: 16)
    types = header_types.types_of(json.must(), wanted)
    sugar = header_types.sugar_of(json.must(), wanted)
    answered @ header_types.answered(wanted, types: types, sugar: sugar)
    check_text = header_types.check_unit(headers, wanted, types: types, sugar: sugar)
    check_c = unit_path(dir: dir, stem: "check", text: check_text)
    publish.write_whole(path: check_c, text: check_text, keyed: true)?
    check_err = publish.private_name(dir + "/check-stderr.txt")
    check_line: [str] @ strings.with_all(list: ["clang", "-fsyntax-only"], more: words)
    check_line @ check_line.push(check_c)

    # The question is the check's, and a header's warning in what clang says
    # is the program's own compile's to tell (defect 248).
    checked = toolchain.asked_of_clang(
        line: check_line,
        what: "checking what the extern out-parameters point at",
        err_path: check_err
    )
    publish.discard(check_err)
    said: str @ "ok\n"

    if checked.is_err()
        said @ failure_note(checked)

    # The record last, after the two files it names by its digest: a
    # reader that finds it finds the answer and the verdict of the headers
    # it lists, whichever build published them.
    kept.write(path: dump_of(dir: dir, sum: sum), text: json.must())?
    kept.write(path: verdict_path(dir: dir, sum: sum, check_text: check_text), text: said)?
    publish.write_whole(path: listed, text: listing, keyed: true)?

    if checked.is_err()
        return fail(code: "clang", msg: said)
    return ok()

# Where the dump's answer and a verdict live: named by the record's digest,
# the headers they came from, and a verdict by the unit clang judged too, so
# two compilers that write the assertion differently never share one.
function dump_of(dir: str, sum: str) -> str
    return dir + "/dump-" + sum + ".json"

function verdict_path(dir: str, sum: str, check_text: str) -> str
    return dir + "/verdict-" + sum + "-" + digest.digest(check_text) + ".txt"

# A unit's C, named by its own text.
function unit_path(dir: str, stem: str, text: str) -> str
    return dir + "/" + stem + "-" + digest.digest(text) + ".c"

function failure_text(failed: ()?) -> str
    match failed
        .ok    => return ""
        .err e => return e.msg

function failure_note(failed: str?) -> str
    match failed
        .ok    => return ""
        .err e => return e.msg

## Tests

# The probe a round would hand the check for this program: its words under
# `under`, its headers, and what they resolve to now (`compiling.probe`).
function probing(t: toolchain.Toolchain, p: containers.Program, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], under: compiling.Compiling) -> compiling.Probe
    return compiling.probe(t.build, under: under, headers: emit_externs.headers(p, s, decls))

# The binding the check exists for, refused, asked twice: the first time
# cold, the second from what the first published.
test "the pointee check publishes its answer and its verdict before the record that names them, and reads them back (defect 134)"
    text = "extern \"math.h\"\n    function frexp(x: f64, @e: i16) -> f64\n\nfunction main()\n    e: i16 @ 0\n    print(frexp(8.0, @e))\n"
    parsed = parse.parse(text)
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
    out = resolve.resolve(parsed.tree, s, parsed.decls, parsed.uses)
    c = checker.check(parsed.tree, out, s, parsed.decls)
    low = lower.lower(parsed.tree, out, c, s, parsed.decls)
    t: toolchain.Toolchain @ toolchain.locate_toolchain(hint: "", fingerprint: "port-test").must()
    t.build @ "build/selftest-pointee"
    assert !process.remove_tree(t.build).is_err()
    none: [str] = []
    first_answers: [pointee_ask.Answered] @ []
    first = check(
        t,
        low.program,
        c,
        s,
        parsed.decls,
        answered: @first_answers,
        probe: probing(t, low.program, s, parsed.decls, under: compiling.Compiling(level: "-O0", sanitize: false, search_include: none, package_flags: none, runtime: t.runtime, source_dir: ""))
    )
    assert first.is_err()
    records: i64 @ 0
    listed = process.files_under(t.build).must()

    # The two units, the answer, the verdict and the record, and the include
    # lines `compiling.reads` asked clang about (defect 168): nothing else,
    # and nothing private.
    assert listed.len() == 6

    for path in listed
        assert !strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".tmp")

        if strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: "/dump.deps.txt")
            records @ records + 1

    assert records == 1
    second_answers: [pointee_ask.Answered] @ []
    second = check(
        t,
        low.program,
        c,
        s,
        parsed.decls,
        answered: @second_answers,
        probe: probing(t, low.program, s, parsed.decls, under: compiling.Compiling(level: "-O0", sanitize: false, search_include: none, package_flags: none, runtime: t.runtime, source_dir: ""))
    )
    assert failure_text(second) == failure_text(first)
    assert second_answers.len() == first_answers.len()
    assert process.files_under(t.build).must().len() == 6

# **A package's `-D` reaches the check, and its verdict is keyed by the words
# it was asked with** (defect 161). The header decides the pointee's width by
# a macro the package defines; the check kept a package's `-I` alone, so it
# refused the correct 64-bit declaration, and after the compiler passed the
# `-D` it replayed that refusal from the same `build/` (measured 2026-10-02).
test "a package's -D reaches the pointee check, and its verdict is keyed by the words it was asked with (defect 161)"
    text = "extern \"wide.h\"\n    function hero_get(@out: i64)\n\nfunction main()\n    n: i64 @ 0\n    hero_get(out: @n)\n    print(n)\n"
    parsed = parse.parse(text)
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
    out = resolve.resolve(parsed.tree, s, parsed.decls, parsed.uses)
    c = checker.check(parsed.tree, out, s, parsed.decls)
    low = lower.lower(parsed.tree, out, c, s, parsed.decls)
    t: toolchain.Toolchain @ toolchain.locate_toolchain(hint: "", fingerprint: "port-test").must()
    t.build @ "build/selftest-pointee-package"
    headers_at = "build/selftest-pointee-package-include"
    assert !process.remove_tree(t.build).is_err()
    assert !process.mkdir_all(headers_at).is_err()
    header = "#include <stdint.h>\n#ifdef HERO_WIDE\ntypedef int64_t hero_num;\n#else\ntypedef int32_t hero_num;\n#endif\nvoid hero_get(hero_num *out);\n"
    assert !write_file(path: headers_at + "/wide.h", text: header).is_err()
    none: [str] = []

    # Without the package's word the header's type is 32 bits wide, and the
    # declaration is refused.
    narrow_answers: [pointee_ask.Answered] @ []
    narrow = check(
        t,
        low.program,
        c,
        s,
        parsed.decls,
        answered: @narrow_answers,
        probe: probing(t, low.program, s, parsed.decls, under: compiling.Compiling(level: "-O0", sanitize: false, search_include: [headers_at], package_flags: none, runtime: t.runtime, source_dir: ""))
    )
    assert narrow.is_err()

    # With it, in the same `build/`, the question is asked again and passes.
    wide_answers: [pointee_ask.Answered] @ []
    wide = check(
        t,
        low.program,
        c,
        s,
        parsed.decls,
        answered: @wide_answers,
        probe: probing(t, low.program, s, parsed.decls, under: compiling.Compiling(level: "-O0", sanitize: false, search_include: [headers_at], package_flags: ["-DHERO_WIDE", "-lz"], runtime: t.runtime, source_dir: ""))
    )
    assert !wide.is_err()

    # The key names the compile words alone: without the link word the
    # verdict already published answers, and nothing new is written.
    held = process.files_under(t.build).must().len()
    again_answers: [pointee_ask.Answered] @ []
    again = check(
        t,
        low.program,
        c,
        s,
        parsed.decls,
        answered: @again_answers,
        probe: probing(t, low.program, s, parsed.decls, under: compiling.Compiling(level: "-O0", sanitize: false, search_include: [headers_at], package_flags: ["-DHERO_WIDE"], runtime: t.runtime, source_dir: ""))
    )
    assert !again.is_err()
    assert process.files_under(t.build).must().len() == held

# **The verdict is keyed by the source's own directory and by what the
# header resolves to** (defect 168). Two programs of one declaring file name,
# which is what the library's own asks are in every program, beside two
# headers of one name: one says `int32_t *` and the other `int64_t *`. In one
# `build/` each is asked of its own header, in both orders; and a header of
# that name that appears in an `--include` directory, searched before the
# source's, is asked again rather than answered by the record of the one it
# shadows. Measured before this repair: the second program replayed the
# first's verdict, an `i32` accepted against `int64_t *`.
test "the pointee check asks each directory's own header, and one that appears earlier in the search, in one build (defect 168)"
    text = "extern \"x.h\"\n    function hero_fill(@p: i32)\n\nfunction main()\n    n: i32 @ 0\n    hero_fill(@n)\n    print(n)\n"
    parsed = parse.parse(text)
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
    out = resolve.resolve(parsed.tree, s, parsed.decls, parsed.uses)
    c = checker.check(parsed.tree, out, s, parsed.decls)
    low = lower.lower(parsed.tree, out, c, s, parsed.decls)
    t: toolchain.Toolchain @ toolchain.locate_toolchain(hint: "", fingerprint: "port-test").must()
    root = "build/selftest-pointee-dirs"
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()
    narrow = root + "/narrow"
    wide = root + "/wide"
    inc = root + "/inc"

    for dir in [narrow, wide, inc]
        assert !process.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()

    assert !write_file(path: narrow + "/x.h", text: "#include <stdint.h>\nvoid hero_fill(int32_t *p);\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: wide + "/x.h", text: "#include <stdint.h>\nvoid hero_fill(int64_t *p);\n").is_err()
    none: [str] = []

    for first in [narrow, wide]
        t.build @ first + "-first"
        assert !process.remove_tree(t.build).is_err()
        second: str @ wide

        if first == wide
            second @ narrow

        for dir in [first, second]
            answers: [pointee_ask.Answered] @ []
            asked = check(
                t,
                low.program,
                c,
                s,
                parsed.decls,
                answered: @answers,
                probe: probing(t, low.program, s, parsed.decls, under: compiling.Compiling(level: "-O0", sanitize: false, search_include: none, package_flags: none, runtime: t.runtime, source_dir: dir))
            )
            assert asked.is_err() == (dir == wide)

    # The source's `x.h` is the narrow one; then the wide one appears in the
    # `--include` directory, which a unit's compile searches first.
    t.build @ root + "/build-shadow"
    assert !process.remove_tree(t.build).is_err()
    beside: [pointee_ask.Answered] @ []
    under = compiling.Compiling(
        level: "-O0",
        sanitize: false,
        search_include: [inc],
        package_flags: none,
        runtime: t.runtime,
        source_dir: narrow
    )
    assert !check(t, low.program, c, s, parsed.decls, answered: @beside, probe: probing(t, low.program, s, parsed.decls, under: under)).is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: inc + "/x.h", text: "#include <stdint.h>\nvoid hero_fill(int64_t *p);\n").is_err()
    earlier: [pointee_ask.Answered] @ []
    assert check(t, low.program, c, s, parsed.decls, answered: @earlier, probe: probing(t, low.program, s, parsed.decls, under: under)).is_err(
    )

# **A probe compiles under the build's level and sanitizers** (defect 167,
# `scratchpad/file-queue/optimize-macro/`'s shape). The header types the
# pointee as 64 bits when optimised and when sanitised, so `@p: i32` is
# right at `-O0` and wrong at `-O2` and under `--sanitize`; in one `build/`
# each is asked of its own reading, and `-O0` after `-O2` is not answered by
# the refusal. Measured before this repair: the `-O2` build accepted it and C
# wrote eight bytes into the four, which ASan reported under `--sanitize`.
test "the pointee check judges a parameter under the build's level and sanitizers (defect 167)"
    text = "extern \"lvl.h\"\n    function hero_put(@p: i32)\n\nfunction main()\n    n: i32 @ 0\n    hero_put(@n)\n    print(n)\n"
    parsed = parse.parse(text)
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
    out = resolve.resolve(parsed.tree, s, parsed.decls, parsed.uses)
    c = checker.check(parsed.tree, out, s, parsed.decls)
    low = lower.lower(parsed.tree, out, c, s, parsed.decls)
    t: toolchain.Toolchain @ toolchain.locate_toolchain(hint: "", fingerprint: "port-test").must()
    t.build @ "build/selftest-pointee-level"
    headers_at = "build/selftest-pointee-level-include"
    assert !process.remove_tree(t.build).is_err()
    assert !process.mkdir_all(headers_at).is_err()
    header = "#include <stdint.h>\n#if defined(__OPTIMIZE__) || __has_feature(address_sanitizer)\nvoid hero_put(int64_t *p);\n#else\nvoid hero_put(int32_t *p);\n#endif\n"
    assert !write_file(path: headers_at + "/lvl.h", text: header).is_err()
    none: [str] = []

    for level in ["-O0", "-O2", "-O0"]
        answers: [pointee_ask.Answered] @ []
        under = compiling.Compiling(
            level: level,
            sanitize: false,
            search_include: [headers_at],
            package_flags: none,
            runtime: t.runtime,
            source_dir: ""
        )
        asked = check(
            t,
            low.program,
            c,
            s,
            parsed.decls,
            answered: @answers,
            probe: probing(t, low.program, s, parsed.decls, under: under)
        )
        assert asked.is_err() == (level == "-O2")

    sanitised: [pointee_ask.Answered] @ []
    under = compiling.Compiling(
        level: "-O0",
        sanitize: true,
        search_include: [headers_at],
        package_flags: none,
        runtime: t.runtime,
        source_dir: ""
    )
    assert check(t, low.program, c, s, parsed.decls, answered: @sanitised, probe: probing(t, low.program, s, parsed.decls, under: under)).is_err(
    )

# **A `cstr` is refused where the header's pointer points at bytes it may
# write, and taken where they are `const`, whatever words clang has for the
# call** (defect 411). Every spelling below, both directions, is judged by
# this check on the real clang: `unsigned char *` and its kin were accepted at
# exit 0 while C wrote the program's shared bytes, because clang tells them as
# `-Wpointer-sign`, and their `const` twins must stay accepted though clang
# warns the same way about them. A golden cannot hold the `const` half while
# that warning stands, so this test holds it.
test "the pointee check refuses a cstr to bytes C may write and takes one to const bytes (defect 411)"
    header = "#include <stdint.h>\ntypedef unsigned char *w_bytes;\ntypedef const unsigned char *r_bytes;\nint32_t w_uchar(unsigned char *p);\nint32_t w_schar(signed char *p);\nint32_t w_u8(uint8_t *p);\nint32_t w_typedef(w_bytes p);\nint32_t w_array(unsigned char p[32]);\nint32_t w_restrict(unsigned char *restrict p);\nint32_t w_char(char *const p);\nint32_t w_void(void *p);\nint32_t r_uchar(const unsigned char *p);\nint32_t r_schar(const signed char *p);\nint32_t r_u8(const uint8_t *p);\nint32_t r_typedef(r_bytes p);\nint32_t r_array(const unsigned char p[32]);\nint32_t r_void(const void *p);\nint32_t r_char(const char *p);\n"
    t: toolchain.Toolchain @ toolchain.locate_toolchain(hint: "", fingerprint: "port-test").must()
    headers_at = "build/selftest-pointee-read-include"
    assert !process.mkdir_all(headers_at).is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: headers_at + "/rw.h", text: header).is_err()
    none: [str] = []
    writes = ["w_uchar", "w_schar", "w_u8", "w_typedef", "w_array", "w_restrict", "w_char", "w_void"]
    reads = ["r_uchar", "r_schar", "r_u8", "r_typedef", "r_array", "r_void", "r_char"]

    for names in [writes, reads]
        lines: [str] @ ["extern \"rw.h\""]

        for name in names
            lines @ lines.push("    function " + name + "(p: cstr lent) -> i32")

        text = lines.join("\n") + "\n\nfunction main()\n    print(1)\n"
        parsed = parse.parse(text)
        s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
        out = resolve.resolve(parsed.tree, s, parsed.decls, parsed.uses)
        c = checker.check(parsed.tree, out, s, parsed.decls)
        assert c.diagnostics.len() == 0
        low = lower.lower(parsed.tree, out, c, s, parsed.decls)
        t.build @ "build/selftest-pointee-read-" + names[0]
        assert !process.remove_tree(t.build).is_err()
        answers: [pointee_ask.Answered] @ []
        under = compiling.Compiling(
            level: "-O0",
            sanitize: false,
            search_include: [headers_at],
            package_flags: none,
            runtime: t.runtime,
            source_dir: ""
        )
        asked = check(
            t,
            low.program,
            c,
            s,
            parsed.decls,
            answered: @answers,
            probe: probing(t, low.program, s, parsed.decls, under: under)
        )
        assert asked.is_err() == (names[0] == "w_uchar")

        # Each writable parameter is its own failure, carrying the marker the
        # reader turns into `ffi_writable_parameter`.
        for name in names
            assert ffi_lookup.has(text: failure_text(asked), needle: ffi_mutable.WRITABLE_MARKER + name + " p ") == (names[0] == "w_uchar")
